#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065435 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2dcb6a2-e8f7-5bb7-9450-e0b65532681a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 867 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
